course: dictionary
lesson: 5254
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word joint can name a part of your body, a piece of meat, or a thing that is shared. Read the words around it to find the right meaning.
explain: «Cats hide pain well, so not jumping onto high places is often the only sign of sore joints.» Jumping and high places are about bodies moving, and sore tells you it hurts, so joints here means the places in the body where bones meet and bend.
explain: «A rolled ankle stretches or tears the ligaments, the tough straps that hold the joint together.» The clue is together: the ligaments are straps that keep two bones joined, so a joint is a place where two bones meet.
explain: «Mill Lane and Station Road hold a joint street party.» Two streets are doing something together, so joint means shared by two or more people. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then test it by putting your meaning into the sentence.
fact: A joint is a place where two bones meet so the body can bend or turn, such as the knee, elbow or shoulder.
fact: A joint can also be a place where two pieces of something are joined together.
fact: A joint is a large piece of meat for roasting, as in «a joint of beef for Sunday lunch».
fact: As an adjective, joint means shared or done together by two or more people, as in «joint winners» or «a joint effort».
fact: Hinge joints, like the elbow and knee, bend one way like a door.
exercise: «Cats hide pain well, so not jumping onto high places is often the only sign of sore joints.» In this sentence, what is a joint? | A large piece of meat for roasting | Shared by two people | *A place where two bones meet so the body can bend
exercise: «Mill Lane and Station Road hold a joint street party.» What does joint mean here? | *Shared by two or more people | Very old | A large piece of meat | Broken into pieces
exercise: «A rolled ankle stretches or tears the ligaments, the tough straps that hold the joint together.» What do the ligaments do? | Make the ankle swell | Cook the meat | Help a robot pick things up | *Hold the joint together
test: «The two teams were joint winners of the match.» What does joint mean here? | A large piece of meat | *Shared by the two teams, so both won | A place where two bones meet | Covered in mud
test: «Hinge joints in the elbow and knee bend one way, like a door.» What does a hinge joint let the elbow do? | Turn all the way round | Glow in the dark | Shake the head | *Bend one way, like a door
exam: «Mum bought a joint of beef for Sunday lunch.» What is a joint in this sentence? | Two people sharing a job | A broken bone | A place where two bones meet | *A large piece of meat for roasting
